{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_01PyWKVjWySXpCyaAJPJWeL9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes! That's the problem. The _clean_dict function is removing all fields that contain \"unknown\" values. But since I'm telling the LLM to use \"unknown\" for missing data, and then cleaning those out, we end up with None for everything.\n\nThe issue is: if the LLM returns all \"unknown\" values, the dictionary gets cleaned to an empty dict, which then becomes None. This is correct behavior - we don't want to show a bunch of \"unknown\" fields to the user. However, the evaluator is checking for the PRESENCE of these fields, not their content.\n\nLet me reconsider the approach. According to the rubric, we get points for having the STRUCTURED fields populated, not just the legacy description field. So I need to either:\n1. Keep the fields even if they're \"unknown\"\n2. Have the LLM provide REAL values instead of \"unknown\"\n\nLet me check what the evaluator actually said about this issue:\n\nLooking back at the EVALUATION_STATE.md, it says:\n- \"The profile LLM is generating good content, but it's stored in `descriptions` array only\"\n- \"The descriptions text contains personality info like 'noble figure', 'bold and aggressive'\"\n\nSo the LLM IS generating good content in the description field. The issue is that this content needs to be STRUCTURED into appearance/personality/voice_guidance fields.\n\nI think the best approach is to POST-PROCESS the profile text to extract structured fields, rather than relying on the LLM to return them in a specific format. Let me implement that:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":909,"cache_read_input_tokens":78565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":909,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sess